:answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464184} {:answer/id #uuid "2a78a5b5-3ebf-52d7-80e9-90e9d11bc53c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464185}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "92158017-308d-5a15-8bfb-50c8e6448c23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464184} :answer-field/id #uuid "8262dd2a-aff8-5c18-b3f8-a27014bc7305" :answer-field/key "${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465026}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1c19bd8b-6180-555e-84ee-4d1df41e83f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-141218" :question/problem "Which of the following integrals have finite bounds of integration and an integrand that is unbounded at an endpoint or an interior point of the interval (improper integrals of the second kind on a finite interval)?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the given integrals.\n\n- Integral ${\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x\\,$ is an improper integral of the second kind since it has finite limits, and \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\to -\\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to {0}^{-}.\n$$\n\n- Integral ${\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\infty }\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x\\,$ is an improper integral over an *infinite* domain since its upper limit is infinite.\n\n- Integral ${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}$ is not an improper integral since it has finite limits and the function under the integral is finite at every point $x\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{4}].$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x\\,$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272247 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092327 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4eab94cb-5a5a-5915-ab95-963cb4c63817" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Closure Properties of Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28dde735-ab59-5aa7-a51d-98ecc8459e63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464186} {:answer/id #uuid "7783da7b-65e7-5ff4-ba7e-14a768333727" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464187}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7783da7b-65e7-5ff4-ba7e-14a768333727" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464187} :answer-field/id #uuid "05f0c02c-7ac4-55c9-bb0c-9086fbf8536b" :answer-field/key "$(x)+(-{x}^{3}-1)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465027} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bbccd430-e74f-5670-b96d-885f34b219c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464188} {:answer/id #uuid "1c48ad2d-2d13-5f75-b5d7-a6fe59e1cfaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464189}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bbccd430-e74f-5670-b96d-885f34b219c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464188} :answer-field/id #uuid "48891abe-6ee9-5807-9e08-fe5ea3058e67" :answer-field/key "$(2{x}^{4}-3)÷({x}^{2})$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465028} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "849cf18a-3fbd-555d-bfbb-0eb5aa35bf63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464190} {:answer/id #uuid "12f96d24-3a0c-5d5b-ae66-b8ac80cf2c7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464191}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "12f96d24-3a0c-5d5b-ae66-b8ac80cf2c7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464191} :answer-field/id #uuid "d5074fa2-6bce-5c64-ace7-821dd564a01e" :answer-field/key "$({x}^{2})-(2{x}^{5}+4x)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465029}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6faa06e9-6d50-5778-8c1a-50c6b3712803" :question/math-academy-id "q-141234" :question/problem "Which of the following expressions are polynomials?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that polynomials have the following closure properties:\n\n- Polynomials are closed under addition, subtraction, and multiplication. This means that the sum, difference, and product of two polynomials is *always* a polynomial.\n\n- Polynomials are *not* closed under division. This means that the quotient of two polynomials is *not* always a polynomial.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our expressions in turn.\n\n- Expression $(x)+(-{x}^{3}-1)$ is a polynomial. It's a sum of two polynomials, and polynomials are closed under addition.\n\n- Expression $(2{x}^{4}-3)÷({x}^{2})$ is *not* a polynomial. Carrying out the division, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2{x}^{4}-3}{{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{2{x}^{4}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}-\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is *not* a polynomial.\n\n- Expression $({x}^{2})-(2{x}^{5}+4x)$ gives a polynomial. It's a difference between two polynomials, and polynomials are closed under subtraction.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $(x)+(-{x}^{3}-1)$; $({x}^{2})-(2{x}^{5}+4x)$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272248 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092327 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4eab94cb-5a5a-5915-ab95-963cb4c63817" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Closure Properties of Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "99ba2c80-c1e8-5c3d-804c-934b93cd821d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464192} {:answer/id #uuid "cc68096b-e1ce-5ba8-80db-8c7afef92695" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464193}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "99ba2c80-c1e8-5c3d-804c-934b93cd821d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464192} :answer-field/id #uuid "b7afe24a-b5d2-58bc-aecc-605baaf14672" :answer-field/key "$(4{x}^{6}-2)÷(2x)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465030} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d33ceea6-5830-5b7f-b9e6-ad7e2fb1a2d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464194} {:answer/id #uuid "94e8502e-1617-534d-9db5-b7e2a07d52f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464195}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "94e8502e-1617-534d-9db5-b7e2a07d52f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464195} :answer-field/id #uuid "29a58fc5-5bff-58bb-a072-7c80657a3f3f" :answer-field/key "$(7x+1)\\cdot (2{x}^{3}+3)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465031} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "75e4b19b-09a4-5fc7-a07f-90460e41a632" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464196} {:answer/id #uuid "f63aee3d-e7c1-51b0-8bf7-2917a1aeb343" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464197}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "75e4b19b-09a4-5fc7-a07f-90460e41a632" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464196} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e56c238-d3a9-5ab4-9919-e4d3273f11ce" :answer-field/key "$({x}^{4}-3{x}^{2}+1)÷({x}^{2})$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465032}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a6242aae-9378-55e8-b137-ddfe7f38d97f" :question/math-academy-id "q-141236" :question/problem "Which of the following expressions are polynomials?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Recall that polynomials have the following closure properties:\n\n- Polynomials are closed under addition, subtraction, and multiplication. This means that the sum, difference, and product of two polynomials is *always* a polynomial.\n\n- Polynomials are *not* closed under division. This means that the quotient of two polynomials is *not* always a polynomial.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our expressions in turn.\n\n- Expression $(4{x}^{6}-2)÷(2x)$ is *not* a polynomial. Carrying out the division, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4{x}^{6}-2}{2x} & =\\frac{4{x}^{6}}{2x}-\\frac{2}{2x} \\\\  & =2{x}^{5}-\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is *not* a polynomial.\n\n- Expression $(7x+1)\\cdot (2{x}^{3}+3)$ gives a polynomial. It's a product of two polynomials, and polynomials are closed under multiplication.\n\n- Expression $({x}^{4}-3{x}^{2}+1)÷({x}^{2})$ is *not* a polynomial. Carrying out the division, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{4}-3{x}^{2}+1}{{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-3+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is *not* a polynomial.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $(7x+1)\\cdot (2{x}^{3}+3)$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272249 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092327 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4eab94cb-5a5a-5915-ab95-963cb4c63817" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Closure Properties of Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ea9c22e6-870f-5511-aef6-136e4451128c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464198} {:answer/id #uuid "2dbd1e14-2c1d-5f25-b6b8-04c918a1e2c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464199}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2dbd1e14-2c1d-5f25-b6b8-04c918a1e2c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464199} :answer-field/id #uuid "8976ca42-08e7-5dde-9133-ee39fa10e558" :answer-field/key "$(4{x}^{6}-x)+(3{x}^{5})$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465033} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9310f0f7-5c56-5fcf-9605-70bfe0e5b86d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464200} {:answer/id #uuid "4534aa5d-783c-5c17-b6ac-cc1bc8de90de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464201}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4534aa5d-783c-5c17-b6ac-cc1bc8de90de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464201} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf98f7f5-3440-57f3-8ae1-ed0738fb2bca" :answer-field/key "$({x}^{3}-1)\\cdot (3-{x}^{2})$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465034} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d5778762-118e-5d67-acf6-70e9fa0eb463" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464202} {:answer/id #uuid "e95057f3-2c7d-5cd7-8da9-6ee8c6c11a6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464203}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e95057f3-2c7d-5cd7-8da9-6ee8c6c11a6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464203} :answer-field/id #uuid "afabd9a0-75c3-52e9-ad82-8fe288c3b975" :answer-field/key "$({x}^{3}-4{x}^{2})÷(x)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465035}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2a3f0c75-73fd-5349-840a-8899dcbd367a" :question/math-academy-id "q-141240" :question/problem "Which of the following expressions are polynomials?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that polynomials have the following closure properties:\n\n- Polynomials are closed under addition, subtraction, and multiplication. This means that the sum, difference, and product of two polynomials is *always* a polynomial.\n\n- Polynomials are *not* closed under division. This means that the quotient of two polynomials is *not* always a polynomial.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our expressions in turn.\n\n- Expression $(4{x}^{6}-x)+(3{x}^{5})$ is a polynomial. It's a sum of two polynomials, and polynomials are closed under addition.\n\n- Expression $({x}^{3}-1)\\cdot (3-{x}^{2})$ is a polynomial. It's a product of two polynomials, and polynomials are closed under multiplication.\n\n- Expression $({x}^{3}-4{x}^{2})÷(x)$ is a polynomial. Even though polynomials are *not* closed under division, the quotient of these two polynomials results in a polynomial:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{3}-4{x}^{2})÷(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{x({x}^{2}-4x)}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{x}({x}^{2}-4x)}{\\cancel{x}} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-4x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $(4{x}^{6}-x)+(3{x}^{5})$; $({x}^{3}-1)\\cdot (3-{x}^{2})$; $({x}^{3}-4{x}^{2})÷(x)$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272250 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092327 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4eab94cb-5a5a-5915-ab95-963cb4c63817" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Closure Properties of Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "75aec792-e88c-5a28-89bc-a1efe948d55f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464204} {:answer/id #uuid "0b5dedcd-6276-5f79-8ea7-ce31b3743b13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464205}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "75aec792-e88c-5a28-89bc-a1efe948d55f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464204} :answer-field/id #uuid "6bd9e6d2-545c-5675-9cdb-faaa03c946f5" :answer-field/key "$(3{x}^{3}-2x)÷({x}^{3})$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465036} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91f074b0-d06c-5d42-a66d-72da93429d3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464206} {:answer/id #uuid "e6a2835c-19dd-5cc1-8b13-cad8ce203017" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464207}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e6a2835c-19dd-5cc1-8b13-cad8ce203017" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464207} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a050e3d-255c-5875-bb32-e8fc2f6cceb1" :answer-field/key "$(10{x}^{6}-4{x}^{3})÷(2{x}^{3})$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465037} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "410ae560-8bb1-5ac7-a76b-5c8caaf29f8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464208} {:answer/id #uuid "af734a28-4311-54bf-b3f3-743583f4ce2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464209}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "af734a28-4311-54bf-b3f3-743583f4ce2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464209} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c5fe96c-fea5-551d-baf7-260402dd95cf" :answer-field/key "$(2x-1)\\cdot ({x}^{3}+6x)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465038}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "78e46443-6b0b-52be-80af-856b64b460d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-141245" :question/problem "Which of the following expressions are polynomials?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Recall that polynomials have the following closure properties:\n\n- Polynomials are closed under addition, subtraction, and multiplication. This means that the sum, difference, and product of two polynomials is *always* a polynomial.\n\n- Polynomials are *not* closed under division. This means that the quotient of two polynomials is *not* always a polynomial.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our expressions in turn.\n\n- Expression $(3{x}^{3}-2x)÷({x}^{3})$ is *not* a polynomial. Carrying out the division, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{x}^{3}-2x}{{x}^{3}} & =\\frac{3{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{2x}{{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =3-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is *not* a polynomial.\n\n- Expression $(10{x}^{6}-4{x}^{3})÷(2{x}^{3})$ is a polynomial. Even though polynomials are *not* closed under division, the quotient of these two polynomials results in a polynomial:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(10{x}^{6}-4{x}^{3})÷(2{x}^{3}) & =\\frac{10{x}^{6}-4{x}^{3}}{2{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{3}(5{x}^{3}-2)}{2{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{2{x}^{3}}(5{x}^{3}-2)}{\\cancel{2{x}^{3}}} \\\\  & =5{x}^{3}-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Expression $(2x-1)\\cdot ({x}^{3}+6x)$ is a polynomial. It's a product of two polynomials, and polynomials are closed under multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $(10{x}^{6}-4{x}^{3})÷(2{x}^{3})$; $(2x-1)\\cdot ({x}^{3}+6x)$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272287 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124521 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9d77214-796e-558f-a364-00ae3e25cc13" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f6e6c5d7-e923-5b79-aeca-92ce53c73074" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449640} {:answer/id #uuid "709274c3-c137-53e6-a5e6-794fdfe9a85a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449641}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "709274c3-c137-53e6-a5e6-794fdfe9a85a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449641} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d40a1ee-aefc-57fa-8071-7deb8e4a3305" :answer-field/key "$T$ is not invertible" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450774} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c7181c58-f5c1-540e-9c79-9a178187acc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449642} {:answer/id #uuid "451e520e-366e-5d15-a3ef-e0067131fc93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449643}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c7181c58-f5c1-540e-9c79-9a178187acc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449642} :answer-field/id #uuid "53ef3a30-34f3-5c51-826c-d16c38c6f3d2" :answer-field/key "$\\text{nullity}(T)=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450775} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8801d71e-6668-5c1b-93c6-53060eac64bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449644} {:answer/id #uuid "e2ff3c94-ccfa-5b45-b2dc-3dd9301a301e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449645}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e2ff3c94-ccfa-5b45-b2dc-3dd9301a301e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449645} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e1e1146-8542-54b0-b796-f3070052fa06" :answer-field/key "$\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 7$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450776}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c894da3b-5f0a-59a9-8060-52795d04ecee" :question/math-academy-id "q-141460" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true given that $T:{R}^{7}\\to {R}^{7}$ is a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T,$ and $T(x)=v$ has no solution for some vector $v?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{7}$ into ${R}^{7},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,\\left\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{7} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=7 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}\\right.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “$T$ is not invertible” is true. Since $T(x)=v$ has no solution, we have that $v\\notin \\text{Im}(T),$ hence $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{7}.$ According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{7},$ the transformation $T$ and the matrix $T$ are not invertible.\n\n- Statement $\\text{nullity}(T)=0$ is false. Since $T$ is not invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0.$\n\n- Statement $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 7$ is true. Since $T$ is not invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 7.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$T$ is not invertible”; $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 7$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272288 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124521 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9d77214-796e-558f-a364-00ae3e25cc13" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a2f591c8-5dc6-5a9b-9834-d348ea0ba55c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/tex