93-a3dd-52f0-a0e9-ed123508f95b" :answer-field/key "$f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability mass function" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465098}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d9521225-8707-5e6c-956f-3108e3ec960c" :question/math-academy-id "q-143868" :question/problem "| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=2$ | $-0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.2$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.4$ | $0.6$ | $-0.3$ |\n\nConsider the function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above. Which of the following conditions is true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's check the given conditions:\n\n- Condition “$0\\le f(x,y)\\le 1$ for all $(x,y)$ in $S$” is false. The values of $f(2,1)$ and $f(3,3)$ in the table does not satisfy $0\\le f(x,y)\\le 1.$\n\n- Condition ${\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}f(x,y)=1$ is true. Adding up all the values of $f(x,y)$ shown in the table, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}f(x,y) & =-0.1+0.2+0.2+0.4+0.6-0.3=1\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Condition “$f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability mass function” is false. Because $f(x,y)$ does not satisfy condition “$0\\le f(x,y)\\le 1$ for all $(x,y)$ in $S$”, it is *not* a valid probability mass function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}f(x,y)=1$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275145 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162063 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "38398622-ca45-5eb6-a589-96aec162960a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Joint Distribution of Two Discrete Random Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32f8e669-a6b6-55eb-b6af-8b945facc904" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464330} {:answer/id #uuid "4738c56c-3016-5759-94d9-d238cfd07380" :answer/type {:db/id #edb