mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ is defined at $x=0$” is true. Using the expression for the middle branch of the function, we obtain that \n\n$$\nf(0)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, $f(x)$ is defined at $x=1.$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Statement ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)$ is true. By calculating the one-sided limits, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}(2x) \\\\  & =2(0) \\\\  & =0 \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}(3x) \\\\  & =3(0) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, the limit of $f(x)$ at $x=0$ exists:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}f(x)=0\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Statement ${lim}_{x\\to 0}f(x)=f(0)$ is false. Indeed, we have that ${lim}_{x\\to 0}f(x)=0\\ne f(0).$  $\\,\\,\\times $\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ is continuous at $x=0$” is false. Since not all statements “$f(x)$ is defined at $x=0$”; ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)$; ${lim}_{x\\to 0}f(x)=f(0)$ are true, according to the continuity test, the function $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$f(x)$ is defined at $x=0$”; ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275312 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064316 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8edb0a6c-f22f-56cf-9545-2fae93896db2" :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Continuity at a Point Using the Definition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25dd7c3c-5e1d-5f4b-8a27-458746ccd605" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464430} {:answer/id #uuid "00c2746a-be60-54e6-8f79-587f24bb51b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464431}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "00c2746a-b