ld/id #uuid "0a73248c-d4f7-5606-8dd7-59415c7496e2" :answer-field/key "![Diagram III](images/a8/a8a0a38e0c05da243e5b8fcdbb22c58517fb98e6dcea4f985756a427183b8c87.svg)" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465203}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4ecd1a30-d61f-5d31-96d2-62fa52545c5c" :question/math-academy-id "q-158029" :question/problem "Which of the diagrams shown below represent subspaces of ${R}^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\nIf none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I represents a set that does not contain the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin, so Diagram I is not a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Diagram II represents a set that does not contain the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin, so Diagram II is not a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Diagram III does not represent a subspace.  In this case, we have that the vector $a=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$ lies inside the given subset. But \n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot a=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nlies outside the subset.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"None\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275441 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192782 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4a8dbbab-2dea-5fff-86f0-3411f5405ee9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether Sets Represented Graphically Are Subspaces in Three-Dimensional Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd96b419-3ee9-5f06-bfb3-c37ca8e5de77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464540} {:answer/id #uuid "06d1f1f0-b376-5f15-8c04-89021b547573" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464541}] 