ficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ec726d7a-9c79-5a1b-91e2-01dc3e4c2650" :question/math-academy-id "q-158102" :question/problem "Which of the diagrams shown below represent subspaces of ${R}^{3}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I represents a plane that passes through the origin. Therefore, it is a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- Diagram II represents a line that does not pass through the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin, so the line in Diagram II is not a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- Diagram III represents the origin $0=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,,\\,$ which is considered a special (trivial) subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is diagrams I and III only"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275472 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105861 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "16a6f0f7-4003-5908-8b1b-68d218fdcb8f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Function Is a Valid PDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "362a535b-6242-540e-8a77-e1d9b6546c1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464558} {:answer/id #uuid "3004f013-258d-57a0-8f9f-075f8e06b17a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464559}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3004f013-258d-57a0-8f9f-075f8e06b17a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464559} :answer-field/id #uuid "031d67b8-58be-59c3-8b17-e3d27630120d" :answer-field/key "$f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465213} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49221aaf-20ee-58dd-9523-3543baa13ce8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464560} {:answer/id #uuid "e1070a12-141c-5c82-970a-b57bd21c6809" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464561}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e1070a12-141c-5c82-970a-b57bd21c6809" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464561} :answer-field/id #uuid "8fa087d1-932f-5250-a483-4d8f937f8557" :answer-field/key "${\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465214} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cbd6590d-4035-5bc5-abf1-4df11f0567e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464562} {:answer/id #uuid "a8322d44-a807-5a8b-9dd9-3142d7cef7a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464563}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a8322d44-a807-5a8b-9dd9-3142d7cef7a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464563} :answer-field/id #uuid "d12fea83-c7a6-5949-a36f-a0a52ab7082a" :answer-field/key "$f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465215}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0f120679-716b-5edd-8658-50352ee4a1a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-158414" :question/problem "![](images/49/495b8aaac849e90ffd46f34f35dd4d3783c2c4fa35c3887c339387d31215d7af.png)\n\nConsider the function $f(x)$ whose graph is shown above.\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+1}{4},\\, & 0<x<2, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement “$f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$” is true. From the graph, we see that $f(x)$ is nonnegative for all $x.$\n\n- Statement ${\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$ is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{x+1}{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+x]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}([\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2}+2]-[\\frac{{0}^{2}}{2}+0]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(4-0) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ is a valid probability density function” is true. Since statements “$f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$” and ${\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$ are true, $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$”; ${\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$; “$f(x)$ is a valid probability density function”."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275476 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105861 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "16a6f0f7-4003-5908-8b1b-68d218fdcb8f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Function Is a Valid PDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "412604d7-85b3-5333-9e30-c595a1008d98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464564} {:answer/id #uuid "ef2731cb-3d3b-5819-a83e-5367bb8a3d42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464565}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ef2731cb-3d3b-5819-a83e-5367bb8a3d42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464565} :answer-field/id #uuid "92f19c20-1204-584b-8e17-9963090d7c2c" :answer-field/key "$f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465216} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11cf68f0-48fb-5dd6-981f-942dc8478d82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464566} {:answer/id #uuid "97831226-9667-5970-8113-5cf6cdbcc885" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464567}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "97831226-9667-5970-8113-5cf6cdbcc885" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464567} :answer-field/id #uuid "35513f5d-2b24-502e-8a30-9f101dbec883" :answer-field/key "${\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465217} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "038e28f2-83af-5f59-9467-b0a45fe3ea4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464568} {:answer/id #uuid "5d31ec98-1a77-5931-9998-9d8811cb4dae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464569}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5d31ec98-1a77-5931-9998-9d8811cb4dae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464569} :answer-field/id #uuid "55795130-2450-5c75-9015-68c2f43ee73b" :answer-field/key "$f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465218}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0ced5dff-e7df-54bc-a45c-2aa3418fd883" :question/math-academy-id "q-158419" :question/problem "![](images/4a/4acac98ef7276cb1e90557ca161666a33ccf0a7627b9ebfcbbe44508b9e5f5bb.png)\n\nConsider the function $f(x)$ whose graph is shown above.\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}x+\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2},\\, & 0<x\\le 1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement “$f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$” is true. From the graph, we see that $f(x)$ is nonnegative for all $x.$\n\n- Statement ${\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$ is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(x+\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[\\frac{{1}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{1}^{3}}{2}]-[\\frac{{0}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{0}^{3}}{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}-0 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ is a valid probability density function” is true. Since statements “$f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$” and ${\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$ are true, $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$”; ${\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$; “$f(x)$ is a valid probability density function”."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278178 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105861 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "16a6f0f7-4003-5908-8b1b-68d218fdcb8f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Function Is a Valid PDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bcc3f3f7-a58b-5346-bd11-154e7ec00b6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464570} {:answer/id #uuid "f3631fc6-16fb-54ad-b7a6-67dc5833b7e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464571}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f3631fc6-16fb-54ad-b7a6-67dc5833b7e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464571} :answer-field/id #uuid "77acc8d6-102d-5dc2-858d-915535ba0021" :answer-field/key "$f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465219} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5fd069fd-2607-50fa-b766-71fb38e3046e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464572} {:answer/id #uuid "2e48bc40-6662-568f-985f-c377f0ee552f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464573}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e48bc40-6662-568f-985f-c377f0ee552f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464573} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d31818b-571a-5cfe-a9b4-5bbd1400880e" :answer-field/key "${\\int }_{1}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465220} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19d5eae6-688d-56ff-87f6-f2e30f55ec41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464574} {:answer/id #uuid "291574f2-4913-56a2-b436-26c002535b6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464575}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "291574f2-4913-56a2-b436-26c002535b6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464575} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e854de9-2c67-5bda-b2c9-6ebb8aa2628c" :answer-field/key "$f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465221}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bac4e6a1-90fc-5ecd-80e4-898747c5b764" :question/math-academy-id "q-158424" :question/problem "![](images/81/819fa17f44f669746ba0c82d2d986f1cd5955a1d7d8144eda6922c0cb7813c9c.png)\n\nConsider the function $f(x)$ whose graph is shown above.\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{15}{x}^{3},\\, & 1\\le x<2, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement “$f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$” is true. From the graph, we see that $f(x)$ is nonnegative for all $x.$\n\n- Statement ${\\int }_{1}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$ is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{4}{15}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{4}}{15}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =[\\frac{{2}^{4}}{15}]-[\\frac{{1}^{4}}{15}] \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{15}-\\frac{1}{15} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ is a valid probability density function” is true. Since statements “$f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$” and ${\\int }_{1}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$ are true, $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$”; ${\\int }_{1}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$; “$f(x)$ is a valid probability density function”."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278179 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105861 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "16a6f0f7-4003-5908-8b1b-68d218fdcb8f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Function Is a Valid PDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b454dc07-632b-5740-8c95-b8159f47d80a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464576} {:answer/id #uuid "83dd29aa-b10f-5ea6-aaa0-9799e0a5247a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464577}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83dd29aa-b10f-5ea6-aaa0-9799e0a5247a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464577} :answer-field/id #uuid "7fcaedbd-6c89-57b2-971f-c9d94f2c00e6" :answer-field/key "$f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465222} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c79e994f-682f-5494-87e4-3b5f8515dcff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464578} {:answer/id #uuid "d84f4c2c-391c-5b55-b5fb-f627e9ce180d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464579}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c79e994f-682f-5494-87e4-3b5f8515dcff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464578} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9d6a385-3da2-5512-bb7e-6dc7800b10f4" :answer-field/key "${\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465223} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ff5f1f28-a8b4-5400-a66e-c3c5ba85ef5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464580} {:answer/id #uuid "a7446b51-8baf-536d-814b-26b6f3a64240" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464581}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff5f1f28-a8b4-5400-a66e-c3c5ba85ef5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464580} :answer-field/id #uuid "294a1be0-119d-5c68-88f5-ab03da770a79" :answer-field/key "$f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465224}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f670c211-dff8-5382-9b62-e560f1a2abfc" :question/math-academy-id "q-158425" :question/problem "![](images/87/87c5e1b3307b2a42fff6a97408c0475dbe242bef4a9dbfbabcec407b3fa23ce1.png)\n\nConsider the function $f(x)$ whose graph is shown above.\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{3},\\, & 0\\le x<1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement “$f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$” is true. From the graph, we see that $f(x)$ is nonnegative for all $x.$\n\n- Statement ${\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$ is false. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}3{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{4}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}(1)^{4}-\\frac{3}{4}(0)^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ is a valid probability density function” is false. Since statement ${\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$ is false, $f(x)$ is not a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278180 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105861 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "16a6f0f7-4003-5908-8b1b-68d218fdcb8f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Function Is a Valid PDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cdd36874-a5b3-5b80-9397-bf4e44707f94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464582} {:answer/id #uuid "84b4cb88-d37a-5d13-9e42-faa7c4544bb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464583}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "84b4cb88-d37a-5d13-9e42-faa7c4544bb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464583} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b856ade-7fe2-5349-a5a5-28bb1ff57ab0" :answer-field/key "$f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465225} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e40683af-6d4d-52b9-b123-d349c1ed7f70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464584} {:answer/id #uuid "278bd59e-8031-5311-b4f7-7e46f395b899" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464585}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e40683af-6d4d-52b9-b123-d349c1ed7f70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464584} :answer-field/id #uuid "7694657f-6b3e-5e1d-8795-49e2cb21539d" :answer-field/key "${\\int }_{1}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465226} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "84082a42-31b8-551d-9a57-581a0ec2cd10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464586} {:answer/id #uuid "04586925-a18f-5497-a4b9-7966350140ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464587}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "84082a42-31b8-551d-9a57-581a0ec2cd10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464586} :answer-field/id #uuid "d5005a3e-0368-554b-b05a-c2142332dc1c" :answer-field/key "$f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465227}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8ba85730-5b8d-5d4c-8799-b386e641d64f" :question/math-academy-id "q-158427" :question/problem "![](images/ae/ae6d25fae4127a74b38bcb9c0de8f44705dca81353c89557047e5d35a3465c5b.png)\n\nConsider the function $f(x)$ whose graph is shown above.\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}2x,\\, & 1\\le x\\le 2, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement “$f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$” is true. From the graph, we see that $f(x)$ is nonnegative for all $x.$\n\n- Statement ${\\int }_{1}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$ is false. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[{x}^{2}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =4-1 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ is a valid probability density function” is false. Since statement ${\\int }_{1}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$ is false, $f(x)$ is not a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278181 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105861 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "16a6f0f7-4003-5908-8b1b-68d218fdcb8f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Function Is a Valid PDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d069b6d-1b28-5ce7-bf94-b37a8e291cb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464588} {:answer/id #uuid "199e07b4-fdc2-520a-860d-5661e00a77c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464589}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8d069b6d-1b28-5ce7-bf94-b37a8e291cb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464588} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4c96219-e0b3-5dc7-8dc7-69c5722fa7b6" :answer-field/key "$f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465228} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ccaefb5b-cfb4-52d2-be52-7e73358d6eb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464590} {:answer/id #uuid "057798e9-f16d-5452-9025-2419284bee6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464591}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "057798e9-f16d-5452-9025-2419284bee6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464591} :answer-field/id #uuid "d855d726-8725-5fca-a081-8879d5d230a6" :answer-field/key "${\\int }_{1/2}^{3/2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465229} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dc175dc9-39c4-5b61-ae58-cabe869e8c8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464592} {:answer/id #uuid "6a338737-2e5b-5530-96cb-fb44a251a025" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464593}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc175dc9-39c4-5b61-ae58-cabe869e8c8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464592} :answer-field/id #uuid "736d1c64-48e3-5924-98c2-79e6482564e4" :answer-field/key "$f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465230}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "50d76297-0f50-5089-b83a-de45e6a36bc4" :question/math-academy-id "q-158428" :question/problem "![](images/6e/6e540dc9309f2a5d1753760b4050c049e380705914d7289b47319de0b0696a9a.png)\n\nConsider the function $f(x)$ whose graph is shown above.\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}4({x}^{3}-1),\\, & \\frac{1}{2}<x\\le \\frac{3}{2}, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement “$f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$” is false. $f(x)$ is negative for some value of $x.$\n\n- Statement ${\\int }_{1/2}^{3/2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$ is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1/2}^{3/2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{3/2}4({x}^{3}-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{1/2}^{3/2}({x}^{3}-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4{[\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}-x]}_{1/2}^{3/2} \\\\  & =4([\\frac{81}{64}-\\frac{3}{2}]-[\\frac{1}{64}-\\frac{1}{2}]) \\\\  & =4(\\frac{80}{64}-1) \\\\  & =4(\\frac{5}{4}-1) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ is a valid probability density function” is false. Since statement “$f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$” is false, $f(x)$ is not a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${\\int }_{1/2}^{3/2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278182 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105861 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "16a6f0f7-4003-5908-8b1b-68d218fdcb8f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Function Is a Valid PDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fcaf39e0-215e-5bcb-9f69-b79d4fea6fd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464594} {:answer/id #uuid "fc883669-b6bd-50ff-84a1-40c6f677f746" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464595}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fc883669-b6bd-50ff-84a1-40c6f677f746" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464595} :answer-field/id #uuid "11957c19-83b4-50d8-b41f-1092eb031c39" :answer-field/key "$f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465231} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "787e3253-5ff2-5136-99a3-45a1fcefd8c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464596} {:answer/id #uuid "7e6eee04-9be6-5e85-a684-d1532f141746" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464597}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "787e3253-5ff2-5136-99a3-45a1fcefd8c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464596} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea00921e-a75c-5d04-a5db-9162d91c392a" :answer-field/key "${\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465232} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1eea68c3-ecab-5537-8997-6369ca24cce5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464598} {:answer/id #uuid "519e2afc-3cba-585d-b51a-f0ae9f20f329" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464599}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1eea68c3-ecab-5537-8997-6369ca24cce5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464598} :answer-field/id #uuid "e686a893-1ac9-5401-87b5-12b786a36222" :answer-field/key "$f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465233}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0715ac14-20ca-5be9-8d09-d1a1ccc33941" :question/math-academy-id "q-158429" :question/problem "![](images/0c/0c30c392223857c77cfc8619f1fa0d4b7fca20af618bdd424570cc3ed712f38b.png)\n\nConsider the function $f(x)$ whose graph is shown above.\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}2x-1,\\, & 1<x\\le 3, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement “$f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$” is true. From the graph, we see that $f(x)$ is nonnegative for all $x.$\n\n- Statement ${\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$ is false. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}(2x-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[{x}^{2}-x]}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =[9-3]-[1-1] \\\\  & =6-0 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ is a valid probability density function” is false. Since statement ${\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$ is false, $f(x)$ is not a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278201 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124641 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "64ba57cc-a3eb-582a-963b-b94895a20bac" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Interior and Boundary Points From Linear Inequalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f970e5b6-748b-5c4c-baed-ac5d23cd85b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449802} {:answer/id #uuid "30d06ec5-0d3e-55a1-b021-5f2fd6f47cd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449803}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "30d06ec5-0d3e-55a1-b021-5f2fd6f47cd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449803} :answer-field/id #uuid "9225bd5d-1a3f-5113-9a58-8af2f115fadf" :answer-field/key "The point $P(2,2)$ lies on the boundary of $A$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450855} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dc2efc61-2ad8-57ee-92bc-c037458de584" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449804} {:answer/id #uuid "f305cd93-59b0-5cc3-9cf2-f44e6f3f3c0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449805}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f305cd93-59b0-5cc3-9cf2-f44e6f3f3c0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449805} :answer-field/id #uuid "eca05e69-25e6-56a1-b759-23098e476289" :answer-field/key "The point $P(2,2)$ belongs to $A$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450856} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b095fc6-eef9-5f6b-8fce-f78d182c6c44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449806} {:answer/id #uuid "2798cbce-ddaf-5a32-a92d-519f34eead10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449807}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2798cbce-ddaf-5a32-a92d-519f34eead10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449807} :answer-field/id #uuid "299aa1ed-32fb-596a-8c5d-345982ea9f21" :answer-field/key "The point $Q(-2,0)$ is an interior point of $A$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450857}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e7714139-ef1f-551b-b703-2555d562d417" :question/math-academy-id "q-158536" :question/problem "The set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:y\\le 2},\\,∂A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:y=2}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $A$ consists of all points that lie on or below the line $y=2.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](images/de/de056be6665a342dc8569e0db330743a96e08fa770798c94ec7dd0cc4e987d92.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(2,2)$ and $Q(-2,0)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](images/c6/c6c1237dfa5f8fed76bad01466c94442d27825c4edab7dbc92a99b177af4a0b6.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “The point $P(2,2)$ lies on the boundary of $A$” is true. The coordinates of $P(2,2)$ satisfy the equation $y=2.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $∂A,$ the boundary of $A.$\n\n- Statement “The point $P(2,2)$ belongs to $A$” is true. The coordinates of $P(2,2)$ satisfy the inequality $y\\le 2.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $A.$\n\n- Statement “The point $Q(-2,0)$ is an interior point of $A$” is true. $Q$ is an interior point of $A$ since there exists an open neighborhood of $Q$ that is entirely contained in $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is '“The point $P(2,2)$ lies on the boundary of $A$”, “The point $P(2,2)$ belongs to $A$”, and “The point $Q(-2,0)$ is an interior point of $A$”.'"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278202 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124641 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "64ba57cc-a3eb-582a-963b-b94895a20bac" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Interior and Boundary Points From Linear Inequalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b4ce51b1-d377-5c86-bbba-c461dce44e9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449808} {:answer/id #uuid "fab814d5-247f-578b-af97-8f677b969195" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449809}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b4ce51b1-d377-5c86-bbba-c461dce44e9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449808} :answer-field/id #uuid "d07e1c45-620c-559e-bfe9-75f701262774" :answer-field/key "The point $Q(2,-2)$ is an interior point of $A$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450858} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e8e12ddb-d9c0-5a9d-be48-0c3878b24310" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449810} {:answer/id #uuid "74fe403a-3b1b-5eee-a47c-0e090dd84ff1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449811}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "74fe403a-3b1b-5eee-a47c-0e090dd84ff1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449811} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b872b1b-04db-5282-9aa6-49e9a771032c" :answer-field/key "The point $P(1,2)$ lies on the boundary of $A$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450859} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a1953b78-ee7a-5ab4-bb0c-5a6ca47eb9ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449812} {:answer/id #uuid "8636fb52-53c0-5dfd-adfb-ff87a594e9e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449813}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8636fb52-53c0-5dfd-adfb-ff87a594e9e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449813} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ad61dfa-e7a1-5a1e-9510-70069ac25a5f" :answer-field/key "The point $P(1,2)$ belongs to $A$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450860}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c0d5bc0a-69ec-5558-bf1a-6901b6354f4d" :question/math-academy-id "q-158538" :question/problem "The set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:x\\le 1},\\,∂A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:x=1}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $A$ consists of all points that lie on or to the left of the line $x=1.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](images/11/119fbce04df572e5b1c083432d0183da1cc04add5e4ad56ac7704af3cf8ee024.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(1,2)$ and $Q(2,-2)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](images/7d/7dc96640505a272ec9701a9d155f7ed2809e6f6df29ac5327af6b2b2324fa162.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “The point $Q(2,-2)$ is an interior point of $A$” is false. Indeed, $Q$ is not an interior point of $A$ since any neighborhood of $Q$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\n- Statement “The point $P(1,2)$ lies on the boundary of $A$” is true. The coordinates of $P(1,2)$ satisfy the equation $x=1.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $∂A,$ the boundary of $A.$\n\n- Statement “The point $P(1,2)$ belongs to $A$” is true. The coordinates of $P(1,2)$ satisfy the inequality $x\\le 1.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is '“The point $P(1,2)$ lies on the boundary of $A$” and “The point $P(1,2)$ belongs to $A$” only.'"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278203 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124641 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "64ba57cc-a3eb-582a-963b-b94895a20bac" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Interior and Boundary Points From Linear Inequalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8dd8d271-7db6-5d9f-98e3-a367b6bf877a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449814} {:answer/id #uuid "15b173ac-5af8-5c8c-afdd-cef94b3e1943" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449815}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8dd8d271-7db6-5d9f-98e3-a367b6bf877a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449814} :answer-field/id #uuid "808f7ea9-3e18-55f4-b934-6094b4f1f5d5" :answer-field/key "The point $P(0,-3)$ lies on the boundary of $A$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450861} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57c89029-9c51-5502-852d-afa07ce23e7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449816} {:answer/id #uuid "52e9af7f-e5ef-55e3-a8bc-9819905121de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449817}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57c89029-9c51-5502-852d-afa07ce23e7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449816} :answer-field/id #uuid "3823a1ea-e9f9-584e-b7c4-74d8eb14b577" :answer-field/key "The point $P(0,-3)$ belongs to $A$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450862} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07fbeb5a-de23-5423-a6b4-837929ce201d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449818} {:answer/id #uuid "f7ac9eb6-db9b-5501-855d-f904069420db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449819}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f7ac9eb6-db9b-5501-855d-f904069420db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449819} :answer-field/id #uuid "79df3794-c500-5eb9-8bf7-60063a07ec0b" :answer-field/key "The point $Q(-1,0)$ is an interior point of $A$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450863}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5cce0d48-0281-56f6-9336-25d5bd51241e" :question/math-academy-id "q-158541" :question/problem "The set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:y\\ge 3x-1},\\,∂A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:y=3x-1}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $A$ consists of all points that lie on or above the line $y=3x-1.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](images/7d/7df3b0c7e884325519ea15f5f949d4b254ef4f63f2449fd36a7aa811ff1b385f.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(0,-3)$ and $Q(-1,0)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](images/7f/7f710e625802403ca5a34f4331cb2998272479c72deca0e24a6116072e4afb5f.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “The point $P(0,-3)$ lies on the boundary of $A$” is false. The coordinates of $P(0,-3)$ do not satisfy the equation $y=3x-1.$ Hence, $P$ is not in $∂A,$ the boundary of $A.$\n\n- Statement “The point $P(0,-3)$ belongs to $A$” is false. The coordinates of $P(0,-3)$ do not satisfy the inequality $y\\ge 3x-1.$ Hence, $P$ is not in $A.$\n\n- Statement “The point $Q(-1,0)$ is an interior point of $A$” is true. Indeed, $Q$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $Q$ that is entirely contained inside the set $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is '“The point $Q(-1,0)$ is an interior point of $A$” only.'"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278208 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124641 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "64ba57cc-a3eb-582a-963b-b94895a20bac" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Interior and Boundary Points From Linear Inequalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60a904b4-7194-50ab-b83e-f7e0e5e93039" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449820} {:answer/id #uuid "d3ef4a6b-c928-5a62-ae02-3e9977a6bcfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449821}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d3ef4a6b-c928-5a62-ae02-3e9977a6bcfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449821} :answer-field/id #uuid "aae8243b-1256-5e62-b9fa-bb6a7c55a17d" :answer-field/key "The point $P(-1,3)$ lies on the boundary of $A$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450864} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f65fb93-3cf7-57e0-8830-ce3492233ae7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449822} {:answer/id #uuid "b809ac26-d305-5728-a7a9-7f1ab5caf2c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449823}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9f65fb93-3cf7-57e0-8830-ce3492233ae7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449822} :answer-field/id #uuid "646ea2a5-95b7-5ab2-937c-f470a17e8df3" :answer-field/key "The point $P(-1,3)$ belongs to $A$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450865} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25716d7c-cbfb-5f0c-901c-3273d61a94da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449824} {:answer/id #uuid "0fc81520-9685-5eff-a9cd-73d8a5e22c39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449825}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "25716d7c-cbfb-5f0c-901c-3273d61a94da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449824} :answer-field/id #uuid "d5765f37-e3df-5178-bc2a-94c5f8fa9643" :answer-field/key "The point $Q(1,-1)$ is an interior point of $A$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450866}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "14446fa2-dc95-59c6-9ff9-236caf0a3198" :question/math-academy-id "q-158562" :question/problem "The set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:y<1-2x\\},\\,∂A=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:y=1-2x\\}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n\\{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta \\}\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $A$ consists of all points that lie below the line $y=1-2x.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](images/7c/7ce1ca6af3caf2b454d7fed484f98d11478839bcf107c0f19562688c7cdaca6a.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(-1,3)$ and $Q(1,-1)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](images/aa/aad8bc7bfd64af0dd1e08618626ae3237bb4aba42be1f91fe7ff9328ad6ecbe7.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “The point $P(-1,3)$ lies on the boundary of $A$” is true. The coordinates of $P(-1,3)$ satisfy $y=1-2x.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $∂A,$ the boundary of $A.$\n\n- Statement “The point $P(-1,3)$ belongs to $A$” is false. The coordinates of $P(-1,3)$ do not satisfy the inequality $y<1-2x.$ Hence, $P$ is not in $A.$\n\n- Statement “The point $Q(1,-1)$ is an interior point of $A$” is false. The point $Q$ is *not* an interior point of $A$ since any neighborhood of $Q$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is '“The point $P(-1,3)$ lies on the boundary of $A$” only.'"}]
